/* ============================================================================
count-primes-below.epher -- how many primes are below n, pi(n)

Counts the primes below a bound with a packed while loop (guide 1.30):
the state holds the candidate and the running count, and the loop is
silent, so only the labelled print at the end shows anything. The count
is the prime-counting function pi(n), written pi(1000) = 168.

Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
ch. 1 and ch. 22; the values pi(100) = 25 and pi(1000) = 168 are the
classic checks. isprime is exact, so the counts are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/primes/count-primes-below.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\primes\count-primes-below.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/primes/count-primes-below.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

The one knob to edit is `bound` below. Everything above the results
section is machinery you can leave alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
bound = 1000

// ---- count the primes below `bound` -------------------------------------------
// v holds {candidate, primes found}; each pass checks the candidate and
// moves on, so the loop stops with v[2] = pi(bound) at v[1] = bound.
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] < bound do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1]) then 1 else 0)}
print("primes below " + str(bound) + ":", v[2])

// ---- the classic small check ---------------------------------------------------
// Same count for a smaller bound, to check the loop shape against the
// table value pi(100) = 25.
bound = 100
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] < bound do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1]) then 1 else 0)}
print("primes below " + str(bound) + ":", v[2])
/* ---- expected output ----
= 1000
= {2, 0}
= primes below 1000: 168
= 100
= {2, 0}
= primes below 100: 25
*/
